  "explanation": {
    "short": "kj and tj make a soft hissing sound, a bit like the \"h\" in \"huge\" (kjøper, tjue). sj is like English \"sh\" (sju), and so is rs in Eastern speech (norsk is NOSHK). sk is a hard \"sk\" in most words (skolen) but \"sh\" before i, y and ø (ski).",
    "more": "Many Norwegians, especially younger ones, now say kj like sj (kjøper like \"sjøper\"). Both are normal and neither is a mistake; Go shows the traditional kj as a soft hiss and the written spelling keeps the difference. The \"sh\" in rs is typical of Eastern speech; in some regions the r is said separately."
  },
